// §4.6.3 + §4.5.6: WHAT THE SMALL-SIGNAL ANALYSIS IS SOLVED WITH.
//
// §4.6.3, first sentence: "A small-signal analysis computes the steady-state
// response of a system which has been LINEARIZED ABOUT ITS OPERATING POINT and
// is driven by a small sinusoid." So an ac analysis needs two things from the
// device and they are different things: the derivative of the residual at the
// operating point (the conductance matrix the sinusoid is pushed through), and
// the stimulus itself. This fixture pins that the two do not contaminate each
// other, using §4.5.6's `ddx` — "The operator returns the partial derivative of
// its first argument with respect to the unknown indicated by the second
// argument, holding all other unknowns fixed and evaluated at the current
// operating point" — because that is the same partial derivative the host
// receives, written where a fixture can assert it. (§4.5.10 is `last_crossing`
// and has nothing to do with `ddx`; an earlier draft of this fixture cited it,
// wrongly, in its prose and in its `//! lrm` line. Both are corrected below.)
//
// §4.5.6 is also strictly the better clause, because its FIRST example is this
// fixture's device verbatim —
//
//     idio = IS * (limexp(V(a,c)/$vt) - 1);
//     gdio = ddx(idio, V(a));
//
// — so the conductance asserted here is the one the LRM itself names, and its
// sentence "If the expression does not depend explicitly on the unknown, then
// ddx() returns zero (0)" is the exact licence for the `ddx(stim, V(a)) = 0`
// check below. §4.5.10 licenses neither.
//
// THE DEVICE. A junction, exactly §4.6.4.5's, biased at 0.6 V:
//
//   id = is·(exp(V(a,c)/$vt) − 1),  is = 1e-14 A, `//! temp 300.0`
//
// WHY $vt IS NOT PINNED TO A LITERAL HERE. §9.15 is the clause that defines it
// — "$vt … returns the thermal voltage (kT/q) at the given temperature" — and it
// supplies NO NUMBER. (§9.10 is Simulator Time System Functions and says nothing
// about $vt; an earlier draft of this fixture cited it, wrongly.) Which k and q
// an implementation uses is therefore implementation-defined in the last digits:
// Annex D.2's default fall-through is `P_K`/`P_Q` = NIST1998, giving
// k/q = 8.617342301212761e-5, while VerA hard-codes CODATA2018's
// 8.617333262145179e-5, and Annex D.2 ships five selectable sets in all. The
// diode exponential amplifies a difference in k/q by V/$vt ≈ 23.2:
//
//   Annex D.2 set   $vt at 300 K           id at 0.6 V              |id - want|/want
//   SPICE           0.025851241113725592   1.2018552965860153e-4    6.81e-4
//   OLD             0.025851260754174377   1.2018341038082657e-4    6.64e-4
//   NIST1998        0.025852026903638286   1.2010077165845230e-4    2.43e-5
//   NIST2010        0.025851997154882865   1.2010397927872850e-4    2.36e-6
//   NIST2018        0.025851999786435535   1.2010369553128491e-4    0
//
// (want = the NIST2018 row, which is CODATA2018 and what VerA uses.) The widest
// spread is 6.81e-4, so the `id` band is 1e-3: it admits all five sets and still
// fails a wrong `is` (a decade), a wrong exponent, or a factor of 2. Against the
// include's own `P_K/`P_Q (the NIST1998 fall-through) the five $vt values differ
// by at most 7.86e-7 V (SPICE), so the $vt identity band is 1e-6 V: a $vt wrong in
// kind (Celsius instead of kelvin, q/kT inverted) misses by more than 2e-3 V.
//
// A fixture that wrote either constant's digits down would fail every
// implementation that picked the other one, and the cheapest way to make it pass
// would be to change the compiler's constants to match the test. So the identity
// is asserted against the LRM's OWN macros instead, with a tolerance that admits
// any defensible constant set (1e-6 V, above) and still catches a $vt that is wrong in kind — wrong temperature,
// q/kT inverted, a missing factor.
//
// The DERIVATIVE is likewise asserted as §4.5.6's identity rather than as
// digits, for the same reason and one more: it is the identity that carries the
// fixture's real claim.
//
//   ∂id/∂V(a) = is·exp(V/$vt)/$vt = (id + is)/$vt
//
// The `+ is` is the whole point of the 1e-15 absolute tolerance. At this bias
// the conductance is ≈4.6458e-3 S and dropping the `+ is` moves it by 3.87e-13
// — the 11th digit — so a tolerance of 1e-15 still separates them, while a
// conductance from a finite difference with any sane step lands further away
// than either. The assertion distinguishes an EXACT derivative from a numerical
// one, and an inexact ac conductance is an ac response wrong by the same
// fraction at every frequency.
//
// THE STIMULUS. `ac_stim` is an INDEPENDENT source: §4.6.3 says it "models a
// source with magnitude mag and phase phase", and a source's magnitude does not
// depend on any node potential. So its contribution to the conductance matrix
// is exactly zero, and the branch's total derivative is the junction's alone:
//
//   ddx(ac_stim("ac", 2.0, 0.0), V(a)) = 0.0        exactly
//   ddx(id + ac_stim("ac", 2.0, 0.0), V(a)) = ddx(id, V(a))
//
// A compiler that folded the stimulus into the linearization — the mistake a
// `mag·something(x)` lowering invites — fails the fourth and fifth checks while
// still reporting the right stimulus value.
//
//! lrm 4.6.3
//! lrm 4.5.6
//! lrm 9.15
//! analysis ac
//! temp 300.0
//! bias V(a) = 0.6
//! bias V(c) = 0.0
`include "check.vh"
module a06_small_signal_linearization(a, c);
    inout a, c;
    electrical a, c;
    parameter real is = 1e-14 from (0:inf);
    real id, stim;

    analog begin
        id = is * (exp(V(a, c) / $vt) - 1.0);
        stim = ac_stim("ac", 2.0, 0.0);

        // §9.15's identity, against the LRM's own Annex D.2 constants. The
        // 1e-6 band admits all five Annex D.2 k/q sets (at most 7.86e-7 V
        // apart here) and still fails a $vt that is wrong in kind.
        `CHECKEQ("$vt is kT/q; the last digits of k and q are implementation-defined",
            $vt, $temperature * (`P_K / `P_Q), 1e-6);
        // Loose enough to survive all five sets (they spread `id` by up to
        // 6.81e-4), tight enough to catch a wrong `is` or a wrong exponent.
        `CHECKR("junction current at 0.6 V", id, 1.2010369553128491e-4, 1e-3);
        `CHECKEQ("the operating-point conductance is exactly (id + is)/$vt",
            ddx(id, V(a)), (id + is) / $vt, 1e-15);
        `CHECKX("an independent source has no conductance", ddx(stim, V(a)), 0.0);
        `CHECKEQ("so the branch derivative is the junction's alone",
            ddx(id + stim, V(a)), (id + is) / $vt, 1e-15);
        `CHECKX("while the stimulus itself is still the declared magnitude", stim, 2.0);

        I(a, c) <+ id + stim;
    end
endmodule
